            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Petitioner (C. Christopher Trower), according to Petitioner (C. Christopher Trower), Kentucky's tax exemption for municipal bond interest paid by Kentucky and its political subdivisions to Kentucky taxpayers treats all private entities the same, but favors Kentucky and its political subdivisions."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Petitioner",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "2b0d9e6ace362e0e1097a10a2efc797ec7c47e50458c9e56fa236e18114a20bf",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-2b0d9e6ace36-0002-0004"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 54,
              "start": 54
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Petitioner, the exemption is equivalent to a payment by the state, with each dollar of exemption representing a detriment to the state."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Respondent (G. Eric Brunstad, Jr.)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "2b0d9e6ace362e0e1097a10a2efc797ec7c47e50458c9e56fa236e18114a20bf",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-2b0d9e6ace36-0000-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 18,
              "start": 18
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Respondent (G. Eric Brunstad, Jr.), according to Respondent (G. Eric Brunstad, Jr.), this case is fundamentally about tax policy, not a monopoly, and Kentucky has not taken over the national municipal bond market."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. Brunstad",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "2b0d9e6ace362e0e1097a10a2efc797ec7c47e50458c9e56fa236e18114a20bf",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-2b0d9e6ace36-0001-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 26,
              "start": 26
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Mr. Brunstad, the Commerce Clause does not prohibit state actions that give residents an advantage unless the action is tied to regulation of interstate commerce; discriminatory taxation is prohibited, but direct subsidies of domestic industry are generally permissible."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Petitioner",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "2b0d9e6ace362e0e1097a10a2efc797ec7c47e50458c9e56fa236e18114a20bf",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-2b0d9e6ace36-0002-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 39,
              "start": 39
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Petitioner, Ohio enacted a similar discriminatory tax scheme, taxing out\u2011of\u2011state bonds unless the state also taxes Utah bonds."
      }
    ],
